          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Fedosov’s method (
            %a{:href => "#Fedosov94"}> Fedosov 94
            ) is a prescription for constructing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantizations"} formal deformation quantizations
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          %p
            In particular this establishes the existence of formal deformation quantizations of all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            %a{:href => "deformation+quantization#Kontsevich97"} Kontsevich 97
            more generally proves the existence of deformation quantization of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            of finite dimension, Fedosov’s method, in a variant applicable to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+K%C3%A4hler+structures"} almost Kähler structures
            (
            %a{:href => "#KarabegovSchlichenmaier01"}> Karabegov-Schlichenmaier 01
            ) generalizes to infinite-dimensional symplectic manifolds as they appear in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            equivalent to the method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ). (Kontsevich’s deformation quantization is however not compatible with field theory (
            %a{:href => "#HawkinsRejzner16"}> Hawkins-Rejzner 16, section 5.3.2
            ).) For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %p Fedosov’s method proceeds by the following steps:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                For each point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                regard the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_x X
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ω
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T_x X, \omega_x)
                and consider its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+deformation+quantization"} Moyal deformation quantization
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒲
                          %mi x
                        %mo ≔
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi Moy
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi x
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi ω
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W}_x \coloneqq A_{Moy}(T_x X, \omega_x)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
                these Moyal algebras are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
                of the underlying
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
              %p
                The union of these yelds an
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒲
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W} \to X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi Moy
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ω
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{Moy}(T_x X, \omega_x)
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of sections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_X(\mathcal{W})
                inherits itself the structure of an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %li
              %p
                Find a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi Fed
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{Fed}
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W}
                which respects the associative algebra structure, and such that there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+isomorphism"} linear isomorphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊃
                      %mi ker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi Fed
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Gamma_X(\mathcal{W}) \supset  ker(\nabla_{Fed}) \simeq C^\infty(X) [ [ \hbar ] ]
              %p
                between the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariantly+constant+sections"} covariantly constant sections
                of the algebra bundle and the algebra of smooth functions on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
                adjoined.
              %p
                Such
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi Fed
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{Fed}
                exists, (non-uniquely), it is called a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Fedosov connection
            %li
              %p
                By this linear isomorphism the algebra structure on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_X(\mathcal{W})
                induces an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                structure on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)[ [ \hbar ] ]
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              %p
                In the context of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                (
                %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
                ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+K%C3%A4hler+geometric+quantization"} almost Kähler geometric quantization
          %h2#references References
          %p The method is due to
          %ul
            %li#Fedosov94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Fedosov"}> Boris Fedosov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A simple geometrical construction of deformation quantization
              %strong 40
              2 (1994) 213–238 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4310/jdg/1214455536"}> doi:10.4310/jdg/1214455536
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p with a monograph account in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Fedosov"}> Boris Fedosov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Deformation Quantization and Index Theory
          %p
            Its generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+K%C3%A4hler+structures"} almost Kähler structures
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#KarabegovSchlichenmaier01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Karabegov"}> Alexander Karabegov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Schlichenmaier"} Martin Schlichenmaier
              = succeed "," do
                %em Almost-Kähler deformation quantization
              %strong 57
              2 (2001) 135–148 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1017993513935"}> doi:10.1023/A:1017993513935
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0102169"} arXiv:0102169
          %p with a survey in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Schlichenmaier"}> Martin Schlichenmaier
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Deformation quantization for almost-Kähler manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 11
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2991/jnmp.2004.11.s1.6"} doi:10.2991/jnmp.2004.11.s1.6
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.atlantis-press.com/php/download_paper.php?id=540"} pdf
          %p
            The observation that the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            is equivalent to Fedosov quantization is due to
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
              ,
              %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Discussion showing that this generalization to field theory is not given by Kontsevich deformation quantization:
          %ul
            %li#HawkinsRejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"}> Eli Hawkins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Star Product in Interacting Quantum Field Theory
              %strong 110
              (2020) 1257–1313 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.09157"}> arXiv:1612.09157
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11005-020-01262-4"} doi:10.1007/s11005-020-01262-4
        .revisedby
